Expense Splitter for Roommates / Groups
A Command-Line Java Application for Group Expense Tracking and Debt Settlement
Submitted by Subhradip2007  |  Java (JDK 8+)  |  September 2026
github.com/Subhradip2007/Expense-Splitter-for-Roommates-Groups-Expensesplitter-JAVA
1. Introduction & Problem Statement
Splitting shared expenses among roommates or groups is tedious and error-prone when tracked manually, often causing more repayments than necessary. This console-based Java application lets a group register members, record shared expenses, view running balances, and — its key feature — compute the minimum number of cash transactions needed to clear all debts, using the idea behind apps like Splitwise.
2. Objectives & Scope
Objectives: design a simple, dependency-free command-line tool for group expense management; correctly model shared expenses and equal-split contributions among selected members; maintain and display real-time balances for every member; implement a greedy debt-settlement algorithm that minimises the number of transactions; and handle invalid user input gracefully using exception handling.
Scope: the application targets small, informal groups needing a lightweight way to track shared spending during a single running session. It focuses purely on computation and console interaction — it does not persist data between runs, does not support unequal or custom-ratio splits, and does not include a graphical or web interface, keeping the focus on collections, OOP design, and algorithmic problem-solving.
3. Technology Stack
Programming language: Java (core language only, no external libraries). Minimum JDK version: JDK 8, with JDK 17+ recommended. Core packages used: java.util.* — Scanner, List, ArrayList, Map, LinkedHashMap. No build tools are required; the project compiles directly with javac. The interface is command-line/console-based (text I/O), with no external dependencies and in-memory-only persistence (no file or database storage).
4. System Design
The application follows a simple procedural-cum-object-oriented design contained in a single class file, ExpenseSplitter.java, combining two lightweight data-holder classes with a set of static methods that implement the menu-driven workflow. Data is held purely in memory for the duration of the program's execution.
Person is an inner static class holding a member's name and running balance, where a positive value means the group owes them and a negative value means they owe the group. Expense is an inner static class storing one recorded expense: who paid, the amount, and the list of members it is split among. The 'people' field is a Map<String, Person> — a LinkedHashMap of all group members keyed by name, preserving insertion order — and 'expenses' is a List<Expense> acting as a historical log of every expense added during the session. The main() method is the program entry point, registering members and driving the menu loop; addExpense() collects and validates expense details and updates balances; showBalances() prints each member's current balance status; and settleDebts() runs the greedy settlement algorithm and prints the resulting transaction plan.
Data flow: the user first registers all group members, each initialised with a zero balance. For every expense, the payer's balance is increased by the full amount, while every participant's balance — including the payer's, if they are part of the split — is decreased by their equal share. At any time, the balances reflect the net position of each member, and when settlement is requested, these balances are used to compute the minimum set of payments that bring every member back to zero.
5. Core Algorithm — Minimum-Transaction Debt Settlement
After all expenses are recorded, each member has a net balance: positive means the group owes them money, negative means they owe the group money, and near zero means they are settled. A naive approach would have every debtor pay every creditor they individually owe, producing many small transactions. This project instead uses a greedy algorithm: split all members into creditors (balance > 0) and debtors (balance < 0); sort creditors in descending order of balance and debtors in ascending order, i.e. the largest debt first; repeatedly match the current largest debtor with the current largest creditor, transferring the smaller of the two magnitudes; record this as one transaction ("X pays Rs. amount to Y") and adjust both balances accordingly; and remove whichever party reaches zero from consideration, repeating the process until all debtors and creditors are settled.
Because each step fully clears at least one person's balance, this greedy strategy settles a group of N unbalanced members in at most N − 1 transactions — the theoretical minimum for a single-currency group settlement, and significantly fewer than a pairwise approach of one transaction per original expense relationship. In terms of complexity, recording an expense is O(k), where k is the number of people the expense is split among; sorting creditors and debtors is O(m log m), where m is the number of unsettled members; generating the settlement plan is O(m) after sorting; and the overall settlement runs in O(m log m).
6. Implementation Details
Project structure: a single folder, expense-splitter/, containing ExpenseSplitter.java (all logic in one file) and a README.md for documentation.
When an expense is added, the payer's balance is credited with the full amount, and the amount is divided equally among the selected participants, each of whom is debited their share (double share = amount / count). Names are validated against the registered member list, and both invalid numeric input and unknown names are caught using exception handling — NumberFormatException and IllegalArgumentException — so the program does not crash on bad input.
The showBalances() method iterates over all registered members and classifies each one as owed money, owing money, or settled, using a small tolerance of 0.01 to avoid floating-point rounding artefacts being reported as outstanding balances. The settleDebts() method operates on temporary copies of the balances so the original running totals used for display are not disturbed by the settlement simulation, then applies the greedy matching algorithm and prints each resulting transaction.
The main() method first collects the number of members and their names, then enters an infinite loop presenting four options — Add Expense, Show Balances, Settle Debts, and Exit — reading the user's choice with a Scanner and dispatching to the corresponding method via a switch statement.
7. Compilation, Execution & Sample Run
To run the program: ensure JDK 8 or above is installed (java -version and javac -version); compile the source file with javac ExpenseSplitter.java; run the compiled program with java ExpenseSplitter; enter the number of group members and their names when prompted; and use the on-screen menu to add expenses, view balances, or generate a settlement plan.
Sample run: in a group of three people — Alice, Bob, and Charlie — Alice pays Rs. 300 for an expense split equally among all three. The resulting settlement plan (minimum transactions) is: Bob pays Rs. 100.00 to Alice, and Charlie pays Rs. 100.00 to Alice.
8. Testing
The application was verified through manual console testing across several scenarios. An equal 3-way split (Alice pays 300, split among Alice, Bob, and Charlie) correctly resulted in Bob and Charlie each paying Rs. 100 to Alice — Pass. An already-settled group with no expenses added correctly showed "Everyone is already settled up!" — Pass. An invalid, non-numeric amount entry correctly triggered a "Please enter a valid number" error without crashing — Pass. An unknown member name not in the registered group correctly raised a "Person not found" error — Pass. Multiple unequal expenses paid by different members correctly netted out balances and produced a settlement using the minimum number of transactions — Pass.
9. Limitations
Data is not persisted; all members, expenses, and balances are lost when the program exits. Only equal splits among selected participants are supported — custom ratios or percentages are not. The application offers single-currency support only, with no handling of multiple currencies or exchange rates. It is console-only, with no GUI and no multi-user or concurrent access support, and it has no authentication or protection against accidental duplicate entry of an expense.
10. Future Enhancements
Planned improvements include persisting data to a file or database so sessions can be resumed later; supporting unequal or custom expense splits (fixed amounts or percentages); adding a graphical user interface (JavaFX/Swing) or a web front end; allowing editing or deleting of previously recorded expenses; exporting settlement plans and expense history to PDF or CSV; and adding multi-currency support with configurable exchange rates.
11. Conclusion
The Expense Splitter successfully demonstrates how a well-chosen greedy algorithm can turn a common real-world annoyance — settling shared group expenses — into a simple, automated calculation. Built entirely with core Java and no external dependencies, the project shows solid use of object-oriented design (Person and Expense classes), collection data structures (Map, List), exception handling for robust input validation, and an efficient minimum-transaction settlement algorithm. While currently limited to a single in-memory session with equal-split expenses, the design is straightforward to extend with persistence, custom splits, and a richer interface in future iterations.
